      "question": {
        ":question/id": "a67649f5-1008-50f7-ae5d-d250a47de9d3",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=-x+5?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-x+5$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-1.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=x+3$ has a slope of $1.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=x+3$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
